WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.715
Back in the warehouse, new boxes arrive all day.

2
00:00:05.715 --> 00:00:12.121
Picture every worker walking to the floor manager's desk with each new box, to ask where it should go.

3
00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:15.476
The queue at that desk would soon be longer than the work.

4
00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:19.477
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

5
00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:23.592
Every new in Java needs memory, and all the threads share one heap.

6
00:00:23.592 --> 00:00:26.172
So doesn't every new have to wait in line?

7
00:00:27.267 --> 00:00:29.601
Here's what the warehouse does instead.

8
00:00:29.601 --> 00:00:32.715
Each worker reserves a stretch of empty shelf, once.

9
00:00:32.715 --> 00:00:38.103
Then every new box goes in the next free spot of their own stretch, without asking anyone.

10
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:39.146
Let's map it.

11
00:00:39.146 --> 00:00:43.461
The workers are threads, the paths of work running through a program.

12
00:00:43.461 --> 00:00:48.088
The new-arrivals floor is eden, the part of the heap where new objects go.

13
00:00:48.088 --> 00:00:52.153
The reserved stretch is a TLAB, a thread-local allocation buffer.

14
00:00:52.153 --> 00:00:55.092
And the next free spot is a pointer called top.

15
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.210
Putting an object in a TLAB means moving top forward by the object's size.

16
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:03.885
That's called bump-pointer allocation.

17
00:01:03.885 --> 00:01:07.476
Here's HotSpot's fast path, from the JDK 25 source.

18
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.313
Does the object fit between top and end?

19
00:01:10.313 --> 00:01:12.992
Then move top, and hand back the old top.

20
00:01:12.992 --> 00:01:14.952
A compare, an add and a store.

21
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:19.492
Then the JVM writes the object's header and zeroes its fields.

22
00:01:19.492 --> 00:01:29.259
Once the JIT, the part of the JVM that turns busy code into machine code, has compiled a method, these few instructions sit right inside it.

23
00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:31.421
A new doesn't even make a call.

24
00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:33.493
And when the stretch is full?

25
00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:34.839
That's the slow path.

26
00:01:34.839 --> 00:01:42.214
The thread goes back to eden for a fresh TLAB, with one atomic update, a single step no other thread can interrupt.

27
00:01:42.214 --> 00:01:45.612
That's the only moment two threads' allocations meet.

28
00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:48.602
How often does that happen?

29
00:01:48.602 --> 00:01:56.266
The lesson's TlabDemo allocates five million small arrays of 40 bytes, 200 MB in all, on one thread.

30
00:01:56.266 --> 00:01:58.411
How many trips back to eden?

31
00:01:58.411 --> 00:01:59.637
Pause and guess.

32
00:02:02.633 --> 00:02:04.698
Here's one JDK 25 run.

33
00:02:04.698 --> 00:02:11.453
By the first collection, the thread had used about 98 MB in 196 refills.

34
00:02:11.453 --> 00:02:17.365
So each TLAB held about 500 KB, roughly 12,500 arrays per trip.

35
00:02:17.365 --> 00:02:22.244
And slow allocs is 0: no array had to skip its TLAB.

36
00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:27.638
Twelve and a half thousand boxes for every visit to the floor manager.

37
00:02:27.638 --> 00:02:30.384
That manager has a lot of quiet afternoons.

38
00:02:30.384 --> 00:02:34.344
So the point is: threads almost never meet when they allocate.

39
00:02:35.433 --> 00:02:39.888
Now here the warehouse picture breaks, and the break teaches something.

40
00:02:39.888 --> 00:02:43.715
In a warehouse, carrying a box to its shelf is the hard part.

41
00:02:43.715 --> 00:02:46.601
In the JVM, the bump costs a few instructions.

42
00:02:46.601 --> 00:02:47.919
The bill comes later.

43
00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:51.586
Every TLAB a thread fills brings eden closer to full.

44
00:02:51.586 --> 00:02:58.557
And a full eden calls the cleaning crew: a young collection, the garbage collector's run that empties eden.

45
00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:04.809
So what you pay for is the allocation rate, the bytes allocated per second.

46
00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:10.079
A service that allocates 1 GB a second fills a 500 MB eden twice a second.

47
00:03:10.079 --> 00:03:17.131
With G1, the default collector, each of those is a pause, a moment when the program's threads stop.

48
00:03:17.131 --> 00:03:18.342
One new is cheap.

49
00:03:18.342 --> 00:03:19.980
A flood of them is not.

50
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.130
Now, sometimes a box never leaves a worker's desk.

51
00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:28.136
This loop makes a Point, adds up its two fields, and moves on.

52
00:03:28.136 --> 00:03:29.880
p is never stored anywhere.

53
00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.758
A Point is a 12-byte header plus two longs: 32 bytes.

54
00:03:33.758 --> 00:03:37.071
So a million of them should be 32 million bytes.

55
00:03:37.071 --> 00:03:42.523
The lesson's demo calls sumPoints ten times, and counts the bytes of each call.

56
00:03:42.523 --> 00:03:43.834
What do you expect?

57
00:03:43.834 --> 00:03:44.939
Pause and guess.

58
00:03:48.633 --> 00:03:51.792
Round 0: about 3.3 million bytes.

59
00:03:51.792 --> 00:03:53.802
Round 1: 1.6 million.

60
00:03:53.802 --> 00:03:55.908
Round 2: 262 thousand.

61
00:03:55.908 --> 00:03:57.248
Round 3: zero.

62
00:03:57.248 --> 00:04:00.693
And zero for every round after that.

63
00:04:00.693 --> 00:04:03.756
Same code, same new, zero bytes.

64
00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:08.044
That's C2, the JIT compiler for the hottest methods.

65
00:04:08.044 --> 00:04:13.564
Its escape analysis checks whether an object can be seen anywhere outside the method.

66
00:04:13.564 --> 00:04:20.513
If it can't, C2 keeps the fields in registers, the processor's own small slots, and never makes the object.

67
00:04:20.513 --> 00:04:22.656
That's called scalar replacement.

68
00:04:22.867 --> 00:04:30.127
In the warehouse, it's a worker who notices a box never leaves the desk, and just writes its contents on the notepad.

69
00:04:31.033 --> 00:04:33.037
But look at the first rounds.

70
00:04:33.037 --> 00:04:37.597
The interpreter, and C1, the quick first compiler, make every box.

71
00:04:37.597 --> 00:04:42.087
C2 only notices after watching the method run thousands of times.

72
00:04:42.087 --> 00:04:44.643
A person would notice the first time.

73
00:04:44.643 --> 00:04:50.032
And with escape analysis switched off, every round allocated 32 million bytes.

74
00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:51.800
So two rules.

75
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:58.403
Escape analysis is a bonus, not a promise: never assert that code allocates nothing in a unit test.

76
00:04:58.403 --> 00:05:04.472
And an object escapes when you store it in a field, an array or a collection, or return it.

77
00:05:04.472 --> 00:05:05.872
It helps tight loops.

78
00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:08.673
It can't help a map full of boxed numbers.

79
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.714
So, does every new wait in line?

80
00:05:11.714 --> 00:05:11.912
No.

81
00:05:11.912 --> 00:05:18.982
Each thread fills its own stretch of shelf by bumping a pointer, and sometimes C2 skips the box altogether.

82
00:05:18.982 --> 00:05:24.069
What costs you is the rate: how fast the boxes pile up for the cleaning crew.

83
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:25.796
Quick check.

84
00:05:25.796 --> 00:05:30.237
Two threads each allocate a million small objects at the same time.

85
00:05:30.237 --> 00:05:32.359
When do they have to take turns?

86
00:05:35.533 --> 00:05:39.007
Only when one of them fetches a fresh TLAB from eden.

87
00:05:39.007 --> 00:05:43.333
That's one atomic update per stretch of shelf, not one per object.
